{
  "slug": "relay-practice-lineups",
  "title": "Relay Practice Lineups",
  "topic": "backtracking-recursion",
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "pattern": "backtracking",
  "aka": "Permutations",
  "order": 4,
  "story": [
    "<p>A swim coach has a freestyle relay team. Each swimmer swims exactly one leg, one after another, and the coach suspects the order matters: a fast starter, a steady middle, a strong finisher. So over the season the team will time every possible order once.</p>",
    "<p>The coach needs the complete list of lineups to pin on the board, with no lineup missing and none listed twice.</p>"
  ],
  "task": [
    "<p>Write <code>allLineups(swimmers)</code>. <code>swimmers</code> is a list of different names. Return every possible lineup: a list that contains each swimmer exactly once, in the order they swim.</p>",
    "<p>The lineups can be returned in any order, but the order of names inside a lineup is the swim order, so it matters. An empty team has exactly one lineup, the empty one: return <code>[[]]</code>.</p>"
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "0 ≤ swimmers.length ≤ 6",
    "All names are different, non-empty strings.",
    "Lineups may be returned in any order."
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "args": [["Ana", "Ben", "Cy"]],
      "expected": [["Ana", "Ben", "Cy"], ["Ana", "Cy", "Ben"], ["Ben", "Ana", "Cy"], ["Ben", "Cy", "Ana"], ["Cy", "Ana", "Ben"], ["Cy", "Ben", "Ana"]],
      "explanation": "3 choices for the first leg, then 2 for the second, then the last swimmer is fixed: 3 × 2 × 1 = 6 lineups. Ana-Ben-Cy and Ben-Ana-Cy are different lineups, because the first leg is swum by someone else."
    },
    {
      "args": [["Mo"]],
      "expected": [["Mo"]],
      "explanation": "One swimmer, one lineup."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "With Ana, Ben and Cy: how many choices are there for the first leg? Once Ana takes the first leg, how many are left for the second?",
    "Build a lineup one leg at a time. For the next leg you may pick anyone who is not already in the lineup. That gives 3 × 2 × 1 lineups, and the choices form a tree: 3 branches, then 2 under each, then 1.",
    "Write a recursive helper <code>fill()</code>. If the current lineup has every swimmer, record it. Otherwise try each swimmer who is still free for the next leg, and for each one fill the remaining legs.",
    "Keep a boolean list <code>used</code> next to the shared <code>current</code> list. For each free swimmer: mark used, push, recurse, pop, unmark. Recording must store a copy of <code>current</code>."
  ],
  "bruteForce": [
    "<p>Generate every sequence of n names where each leg may be anyone, including repeats, and keep only the ones without a repeated name. That is nⁿ sequences to make the n! valid ones: 46,656 for 6 swimmers to find 720 lineups, and 10 billion for 10 swimmers to find 3.6 million.</p>",
    "<p>The waste comes from noticing a repeat only at the end. If Ana is already in the lineup, every sequence that picks Ana again is dead from that moment, so do not build it at all.</p>"
  ],
  "signature": {
    "function": "allLineups",
    "params": [
      {"name": "swimmers", "type": "string[]"}
    ],
    "returns": "string[][]"
  },
  "starter": {
    "js": [
      "/**",
      " * @param {string[]} swimmers  different names",
      " * @return {string[][]} every swim order, in any order",
      " */",
      "function allLineups(swimmers) {",
      "  // Your code here",
      "}"
    ],
    "py": [
      "def all_lineups(swimmers: list[str]) -> list[list[str]]:",
      "    # Your code here",
      "    pass"
    ]
  },
